# coloredcow-notes

Some thinking on a problem I found while going through ColoredCow's hiring process.
Writing this out here so the thinking's visible as it develops, not just the finished answer.

## The problem, in my own words

ColoredCow doesn't have a place where proof of what the company actually is
sticks around and builds on itself. It shows up, then it resets.

A few examples I actually found, not guessing:

- The real story of how ColoredCow works lives on Prateek's personal LinkedIn,
  not anything the company owns. Good stuff, but it's tied to one person's feed.
- The team pages on the site are old and written in third person. Whatever's
  real about the people who work there isn't in them anymore.
- Story Club existed for about a year, real people telling real stories every
  week, and it actually worked, people's confidence changed. But it stayed in
  the room. Nothing carried it past that room, so when it stopped, nothing was
  left outside except an old YouTube channel nobody's watching.
- Hiring posts get written one at a time, whoever's writing that one, whenever
  it's needed. Each one starts from zero. Nothing from the last one carries
  into the next.

So the pattern's the same every time: something real gets made, it lives in
one person, one room, or one week, and then it's gone. Nothing compounds.

## Why it actually matters

If hiring is the thing that drives everything else here, like Prateek said,
then this isn't just a nice-to-have fix. It's a ceiling. Every time someone's
pitching ColoredCow, to a candidate, to anyone, they're doing it from scratch,
depending entirely on whether that one person is good at it that day. That
caps how well and how fast the company can actually hire.

More thinking coming as I work through what I'd actually do about it.
## Possible first moves

A few different ways to actually go after "nothing compounds."

**1. Restart Story Club, but build the outward part in from day one**

The ritual itself worked, people's confidence genuinely changed. So don't
reinvent it, just don't let it be a closed room again. Every session, one
person opts in to have their story lightly edited and put somewhere the
company owns.

Good: uses something that's already proven to work internally, not starting
from zero.
Risk: it already died once. If nobody's specifically responsible for the
outward part this time, it just repeats the same failure in a new shape.

**2. Build a small, boring, owned archive, independent of Story Club**

Don't wait on reviving a ritual. Start a simple page, maybe just a section
on the company site, where any real story, from anyone, whenever it happens,
gets logged with their consent. Slower to fill up, but it doesn't depend on
one format or one room working.

Good: doesn't depend on Story Club coming back to life, more flexible.
Risk: without a ritual generating material regularly, it might just sit
empty. Motivation has to come from somewhere.

**3. Start from what's already proven: Prateek's own LinkedIn**

Instead of generating new material, go back through what's already worked,
his personal posts about how ColoredCow actually operates, and with his
input, adapt a few into things the company itself owns and can keep saying,
consistently, without him being the one saying it every time.

Good: fastest, the material already exists and it's already good.
Risk: doesn't fix the actual root problem. It's treating a symptom, not
building something that keeps generating new proof on its own going forward.

## The one I'd actually make

Not picking one clean option out of the three. Two and three both point at
the same thing, ownership, and one and two both point at the same thing,
where does new material keep coming from. So the real move is combining
what each gets right and skipping what each gets wrong on its own.

Bring Story Club back, because it's the only thing here that's actually
proven to work, real people, real change, not a guess. But this time, from
day one, someone specific owns carrying it outward, not as an extra task
someone might get to, an actual part of the role. And whatever comes out of
it doesn't live on a YouTube channel nobody's watching, it goes into one
place the company owns and keeps building, the same place regardless of
whether Story Club is having a good month or a quiet one.

That fixes why each one alone doesn't fully work. Story Club alone dies
again if nobody owns the outward part, same as before. An archive alone
sits empty without something regularly feeding it. Together, one supplies
the material, the other makes sure it doesn't disappear the moment the room
goes quiet.

First real step, if I started tomorrow: not relaunching anything yet. Go
find whoever's still around from the original Story Club, not to ask why it
died, I already have a read on that, but to ask what would make them
actually want to do it again, and what they'd need to feel safe having it
go beyond the room this time. Build it with the people who already lived
it, not around them.
